## When to invoke

- Building or modifying UI components, pages, layouts, or routing.
- Client-side state, data fetching, forms, validation, optimistic updates.
- Styling, theming, responsive behavior, animation.
- Frontend build configuration (bundler, dev server, env handling).
- Frontend tests — component, integration, visual.

## Component design

- A component renders. State, fetching, and side effects live as close to where they're needed as possible — lift only when a second consumer appears, not preemptively.
- Three similar components are fine. Don't abstract a base until you have a real third use case driving it.
- Props describe what the component needs, not how the caller arranges its world. No `style`/`className` prop drilling unless the wrapping component owns layout.
- Compound components (`<Tabs>`/`<Tabs.Panel>`) beat boolean explosion (`<Tabs hasIcons withBadges variant="pills">`).

## State

- Server state and client state are different problems. Use a data-fetching library for server state (React Query, SWR, RTK Query). Don't reinvent caching with `useEffect` + `useState`.
- Form state has its own libraries for a reason. Hand-rolled `useState` per field works for two fields, breaks at five.
- Avoid global state for things that aren't actually global. A page's filter UI doesn't need to live in a store.
- Derived state is computed, not stored. If `B` is always a function of `A`, don't keep `B` in state.

## Data fetching

- Show loading and error states explicitly. A blank screen during fetch is a bug, not a default.
- Network failures need a retry path. Either automatic (the data library) or a visible "try again" — never a dead-end blank.
- Treat the API as untrusted. Validate shape at the boundary if the backend isn't sharing types.
- Cancel in-flight requests on unmount or when the inputs change. AbortController is fine.

## Styling

- Match the project's existing system (Tailwind, CSS Modules, styled-components, vanilla). Don't introduce a parallel one.
- Use design tokens (CSS variables, theme objects) over hardcoded values. New magic numbers in the diff are a smell.
- Layout primitives (Stack, Cluster, Grid) beat one-off flex/grid declarations scattered across files.
- Mobile-first when responsive matters. Don't ship a desktop-only layout and "we'll do mobile later."

## Performance

- Bundle splitting at route boundaries by default. Lazy-load anything not on the critical path.
- Images need `width` and `height` attributes or aspect-ratio CSS — prevents layout shift.
- Don't fetch in render. `useEffect` for fetches is acceptable; data libraries are better.
- Lists over ~100 items need virtualization. Below that, premature.

## Anti-patterns

- `useEffect` running on every render because dependencies are wrong. If you need exhaustive-deps, listen to it.
- Mutating props or state directly. Even when "it works", it disables React's bailouts.
- `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with anything that isn't trusted, sanitized, and reviewed.
- Calling hooks conditionally or in loops. The rules of hooks exist; lint enforces them.

## Out of scope

- Backend routes, server-side data access, infrastructure — defer to backend.
- Accessibility deep work — defer to the a11y agent. Frontend still owns the basics (semantic HTML, keyboard reachability).
- Build-tool rewrites (swap bundler, swap framework) — propose first, don't unilateral.
